Chapter: Lz77

 THE CHAIN IS A HINT, NOT A FACT. Every candidate it offers is verified by
 comparing the actual bytes, so a chain that is stale, truncated or wrong costs
 RATIO and never correctness. That is what makes the two cheap bounds below safe:
 the walk gives up after lz77-chain-limit candidates, and it stops if a link ever
 fails to point strictly backwards. Neither can produce a wrong match; both stop
 a pathological input from turning the search quadratic again.

 We say:

Section: Constants

 The window is RFC 1951's. THE LONGEST MATCH IS NO LONGER A CONSTANT, because
 serving both formats from the tighter of the two limits was costing Brotli most
 of its ratio: 258 is Deflate's exact maximum and Brotli's is 16779333, so a
 periodic input was being cut into a copy command every 258 bytes where one long
 copy would do. Measured on the interop corpus, the 78000-byte cross-region case
 came to 933 bytes against .NET's 170, and roughly 271 needless commands is where
 that difference lived.

  lz77-window-size : Integer = 32768
  lz77-window-mask : Integer = 32767
  lz77-min-match : Integer = 3
  lz77-max-match : Integer = 258

  lz77-hash-size : Integer = 16384
  lz77-hash-mask : Integer = 16383

 How many candidates a position will look at before settling for the best it has
 found. Repetitive input can otherwise put every earlier position in one chain,
 which is the quadratic case the old scan had by construction.

  lz77-chain-limit : Integer = 128

 At a 255-byte window it could not do much harm: nothing was far enough away for
 the distance to cost much. At 32768 it can, and it did. On input drawn from a
 sixteen-symbol alphabet a literal is worth four bits, so a four-byte match
 reaching two thousand bytes back replaces sixteen bits of literals with twenty
 odd bits of codes, and a mixed test case grew by THREE PER CENT the moment the
 window was widened. Every case with byte-valued literals improved at the same
 time, which is what makes this a pricing question and not a bug.

  lz77-match-overhead : Integer = 12

Section: Token Type
  Lz77Token =
    | Lz77Literal (Integer)
    | Lz77Match (Integer) (Integer)

Section: The Hash Chain

 Both tables are MUTATED IN PLACE. list-set-at writes the slot and hands back the
 same list, so inserting threads only head through the recursion while prev is
 updated through the reference it was given. That is deliberate and it is why a
 position costs two stores rather than two copied tables, but it means these
 tables must never be shared between two tokenizations: lz77-new-table builds a
 fresh pair every time, and nothing here holds one across a call.

  lz77-new-table : Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-new-table (n) = lz77-fill n 0 []

  lz77-fill : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-fill (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else lz77-fill n (i + 1) (list-push acc (0 - 1))

  lz77-hash-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lz77-hash-at (input) (pos) (len) =
    if pos + 2 >= len then 0 - 1
    else bit-and (bit-xor (bit-xor (bit-shl (list-at input pos) 8) (bit-shl (list-at input (pos + 1)) 4)) (list-at input (pos + 2))) lz77-hash-mask

  lz77-insert : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-insert (input) (pos) (len) (head) (prev) =
    let h = lz77-hash-at input pos len
    in if h < 0 then head
    else let p2 = list-set-at prev (bit-and pos lz77-window-mask) (list-at head h)
    in list-set-at head h pos

  lz77-insert-run : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-insert-run (input) (pos) (count) (len) (head) (prev) =
    if count <= 0 then head
    else lz77-insert-run input (pos + 1) (count - 1) len (lz77-insert input pos len head prev) prev

Section: Compression

  lz77-raw-lit-bits : Integer = 64

  lz77-compress : List Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-compress (input) =
    lz77-compress-loop input 0 (list-length input) [] (lz77-new-table lz77-hash-size) (lz77-new-table lz77-window-size)

  lz77-compress-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-compress-loop (input) (pos) (len) (acc) (head) (prev) =
    if pos >= len then acc
    else when lz77-find-lazy input pos len head prev lz77-raw-lit-bits lz77-max-match
      is Lz77Match (off) (mlen) ->
        lz77-compress-loop input (pos + mlen) len (acc & [1, off, mlen]) (lz77-insert-run input pos mlen len head prev) prev
      is Lz77Literal (b) ->
        lz77-compress-loop input (pos + 1) len (list-push acc 0 & [b]) (lz77-insert input pos len head prev) prev

  lz77-find-match : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Lz77Token
  lz77-find-match (input) (pos) (len) (head) (prev) (lit-bits) (cap) =
    let h = lz77-hash-at input pos len
    in if h < 0 then Lz77Literal (list-at input pos)
    else lz77-walk input pos len prev (list-at head h) 0 0 0 lit-bits cap

 The walk stops on any of four things: the chain ends, it has looked at enough
 candidates, the next candidate is outside the window, or a link fails to point
 strictly backwards. The last is the one that is not about ratio: prev is indexed
 modulo the window, so a slot can be recycled under a chain that still points at
 it, and without this test a recycled slot could make the walk circle forever.
 Insisting each step move strictly earlier makes the walk finite whatever the
 tables hold.

  lz77-walk : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Lz77Token
  lz77-walk (input) (pos) (len) (prev) (cand) (steps) (best-off) (best-len) (lit-bits) (cap) =
    if cand < 0 then lz77-settle input pos best-off best-len lit-bits
    else if steps >= lz77-chain-limit then lz77-settle input pos best-off best-len lit-bits
    else if pos - cand > lz77-window-size then lz77-settle input pos best-off best-len lit-bits
    else let mlen = lz77-match-length input cand pos len 0 cap
    in if mlen >= cap then Lz77Match (pos - cand) cap
    else let off2 = if mlen > best-len then cand else best-off
    in let len2 = if mlen > best-len then mlen else best-len
    in let nxt = list-at prev (bit-and cand lz77-window-mask)
    in if nxt >= cand then lz77-settle input pos off2 len2 lit-bits
    else lz77-walk input pos len prev nxt (steps + 1) off2 len2 lit-bits cap

 It is measured LOCALLY, over the next few thousand bytes, and not once over the
 whole input. That is not a refinement; it is the difference between the estimate
 working and doing nothing. An input whose halves differ -- a stretch of
 sixteen-symbol data followed by a stretch of noise, which is exactly the shape
 that showed the problem -- has 256 distinct bytes taken as a whole, so a single
 global reading reports eight bits everywhere and prices the cheap half wrong. It
 was measured that way first and moved the answer by two bytes.

  lz77-est-window : Integer = 4096

  lz77-log2 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lz77-log2 (v) (acc) = if v <= 1 then acc else lz77-log2 (v / 2) (acc + 1)

  lz77-count-distinct : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lz77-count-distinct (input) (i) (len) (seen) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let b = list-at input i
    in if list-at seen b == 0 then lz77-count-distinct input (i + 1) len (list-set-at seen b 1) (acc + 1)
    else lz77-count-distinct input (i + 1) len seen acc

  lz77-lit-bits-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lz77-lit-bits-at (input) (pos) (len) =
    let e = if pos + lz77-est-window > len then len else pos + lz77-est-window
    in let d = lz77-count-distinct input pos e (lz77-zeros 256 0 []) 0
    in let b = lz77-log2 d 0
    in if b < 1 then 1 else b

  lz77-zeros : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-zeros (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else lz77-zeros n (i + 1) (list-push acc 0)

  lz77-worth : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  lz77-worth (mlen) (dist) (lit-bits) =
    mlen * lit-bits > lz77-match-overhead + lz77-log2 dist 0

  lz77-settle : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Lz77Token
  lz77-settle (input) (pos) (best-off) (best-len) (lit-bits) =
    if best-len < lz77-min-match then Lz77Literal (list-at input pos)
    else if lz77-worth best-len (pos - best-off) lit-bits then Lz77Match (pos - best-off) best-len
    else Lz77Literal (list-at input pos)

  lz77-find-lazy : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Lz77Token
  lz77-find-lazy (input) (pos) (len) (head) (prev) (lit-bits) (cap) =
    when lz77-find-match input pos len head prev lit-bits cap
      is Lz77Literal (b) -> Lz77Literal b
      is Lz77Match (off) (mlen) ->
        if mlen >= cap then Lz77Match off mlen
        else if pos + 1 >= len then Lz77Match off mlen
        else lz77-lazy-pick input pos len head prev off mlen lit-bits cap

  lz77-lazy-pick : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Lz77Token
  lz77-lazy-pick (input) (pos) (len) (head) (prev) (off) (mlen) (lit-bits) (cap) =
    when lz77-find-match input (pos + 1) len head prev lit-bits cap
      is Lz77Literal (b2) -> Lz77Match off mlen
      is Lz77Match (off2) (mlen2) ->
        if mlen2 > mlen then Lz77Literal (list-at input pos) else Lz77Match off mlen

  lz77-match-length : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lz77-match-length (input) (a) (b) (len) (count) (cap) =
    if count >= cap then count
    else if b + count >= len then count
    else if list-at input (a + count) == list-at input (b + count) then lz77-match-length input a b len (count + 1) cap
    else count

Section: Decompression
  lz77-decompress : List Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-decompress (tokens) = lz77-decompress-loop tokens 0 (list-length tokens) []

  lz77-decompress-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-decompress-loop (tokens) (i) (tlen) (output) =
    if i >= tlen then output
    else let tag = list-at tokens i
    in if tag == 0 then
      if i + 1 >= tlen then output
      else lz77-decompress-loop tokens (i + 2) tlen (list-push output (list-at tokens (i + 1)))
    else if tag == 1 then
      if i + 2 >= tlen then output
      else let off = list-at tokens (i + 1)
      in let mlen = list-at tokens (i + 2)
      in let start = list-length output - off
      in if off <= 0 then output
      else if start < 0 then output
      else if mlen > lz77-max-match then output
      else let expanded = lz77-copy-match output start mlen
      in lz77-decompress-loop tokens (i + 3) tlen expanded
    else lz77-decompress-loop tokens (i + 1) tlen output

  lz77-copy-match : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  lz77-copy-match (output) (src) (remaining) =
    if remaining <= 0 then output
    else let byte = list-at output src
    in lz77-copy-match (list-push output byte) (src + 1) (remaining - 1)

Section: Queries
  lz77-ratio : List Integer, List Integer -> Text
  lz77-ratio (original) (compressed) =
    let orig-len = list-length original
    in let comp-len = list-length compressed
    in show orig-len & "->" & show comp-len

